\description{
Likelihood ratio test of whether several taxa share the same upper
end-point, such as the extinction of all of them in one event, and the
confidence interval for the common end-point (Solow, 1996). Each taxon's
finds are taken to be a stationary Poisson process, so that, given their
number, they are independent uniform draws over the unknown range of the
taxon. The test improves on the conservative test of Strauss & Sadler
(1989) because its significance level is known exactly.
}
\details{
For taxon \code{j} with \code{n[j]} finds, lowest find \code{L[j]}, highest find \code{H[j]},
and \code{Z} the highest find of all, let
\code{Q[j] = ((H[j] - L[j]) / (Z - L[j]))^(n[j] - 1)}. The likelihood ratio
statistic, conditional on the lowest find of each taxon, is the product of
the \code{Q[j]}, and under the null hypothesis of a common upper end-point
\verb{-2 log(product)} has a chi-square distribution with \code{2 (k - 1)} degrees of
freedom (Hogg, 1956), for \code{k} taxa; with two taxa the statistic is itself
the p-value. The confidence interval for the common end-point \code{U}, assuming
it holds, is \verb{(Z, U_u)}, where \code{U_u} is the value at which \verb{-2 log} of the
product of \code{((H[j] - L[j]) / (U - L[j]))^(n[j] - 1)} equals the upper \code{alpha}
quantile of the chi-square distribution with \verb{2 k} degrees of freedom; it is
found numerically. For one taxon it is the interval of \code{\link[=strauss1989]{strauss1989()}}.

The upper end-point is the one at the largest time. For a common lower
end-point (for instance a common time of first appearance) use
\code{end = "lower"}, which, as the paper recommends, multiplies the locations by
minus one. The paper's assumption of independent stationary Poisson
processes is crucial and should be checked (see \code{\link[=vogel2009]{vogel2009()}}); the
interval is conditional on a common end-point and becomes too narrow if one
taxon ended earlier than the others.
}
